  absolute error: mean 16.55 vs 26.03, first lower on 20 candidates / second on 11 / 2 equal, Wilcoxon p=0.0435 (stat 145.0)

rag, dense (LLM) - rag, tfidf (no LLM)  [MIXED retriever, quoted with care]
  drho +0.364, bootstrap 95% [-0.043, +0.762], P(first ranks better)=0.958, bootstrap two-sided p=0.0836
  absolute error: mean 16.55 vs 54.48, first lower on 27 candidates / second on 5 / 1 equal, Wilcoxon p=3.3e-05 (stat 42.0)
What this does and does not license:
 - A permutation p-value below 0.05 means the ordering is unlikely to be a lucky shuffle of these labels; it says nothing about real hiring outcomes.
 - P(first ranks better) near 0.5 with an interval across zero is the honest form of 'the embedders are indistinguishable here'.
 - The paired two-sided p is a bootstrap tail-area (2 x the smaller tail), not a randomisation test of the difference; the interval is the number to read.
 - Wilcoxon compares the two scorers candidate by candidate on absolute error. It asks a different question from rho - accuracy, not ordering - and can disagree.
 - The 'no LLM' rows are not starved inputs: RetrievalOnlyLLM scores every requirement by its best-matching chunk, while the RAG prompt carries only the budgeted passages. Where the heuristic matches the LLM, that is the result.
 - Repeating a run at temperature=0 is not a resample: the pairs in ARTIFACTS.md agree exactly, so they add no width and no significance to anything below.
